          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Make the tsuyu. Pour the mirin into a small saucepan and bring to a brief boil over medium heat to cook off the alcohol. This takes about 1 minute."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the dashi and soy sauce to the pan. Bring back to a simmer, then remove from the heat. Cool completely, then refrigerate until needed."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Bring a large saucepan of unsalted water to a rolling boil. Add the soba noodles and stir immediately."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Cook according to the packet instructions, usually 4 to 5 minutes. Test a noodle. It should be tender but still have a faint bite in the centre."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Drain the noodles into a colander. Transfer immediately to a large bowl of cold water and ice cubes. Rub the noodles between your palms to remove surface starch."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Drain well and pile the noodles loosely on plates or zaru baskets. Scatter the nori strips over the top."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Pour the cold tsuyu into small dipping bowls. Add sliced spring onions and a small amount of wasabi to the dipping sauce. Serve immediately."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "attrs": {
          "items": [
            {
              "symptom": "The noodles clump together on the plate",
              "cause": "Surface starch was not removed during rinsing",
              "fix": "Rub the noodles vigorously between your palms under cold running water for at least 30 seconds. Drain thoroughly before plating."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "The tsuyu is too salty or too strong",
              "cause": "The ratio of soy sauce to dashi is off, or the dashi was underseasoned",
              "fix": "Taste the tsuyu and add a little more cold dashi to dilute it. The sauce should be strong but not overwhelming when the noodles are dipped briefly."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "The noodles are mushy",
              "cause": "They were overcooked or left too long in warm water",
              "fix": "Soba cooks quickly. Check 1 minute before the packet time. Move them to ice water the moment they are done. Eat promptly."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Zaru soba is associated with summer in Japan. It appears on restaurant menus and home tables from June through August, when a hot bowl of noodles is the last thing anyone wants. Soba noodles have been eaten in Japan for several hundred years, and cold serving methods developed as a way to showcase the delicate nutty flavour of good buckwheat. The dish is eaten quickly once it is assembled. Noodles left to sit absorb moisture and lose their texture."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}